// @run
// @log 42
// @log 63
// @log 30
// @log 64
// The shape the COMPILER ITSELF now uses: `mid` imports one name from `home` for its
// own use, and republishes two OTHER names from that same module — one of them in a
// second `export … from` statement placed after code, and one that `mid` also calls.
//
// Three separate things have to hold at once. (1) An `import { … } from "./home"` and
// an `export { … } from "./home"` in one module are not a duplicate module edge or a
// duplicate binding. (2) A re-export binds the name LOCALLY too, so `midCallsReexported`
// can call `alsoPassed` without importing it. (3) A sibling asking `mid` for a name only
// `home` declares resolves through the merge.
//
// This is what `compiler/emit_base.vl` does with the seven type-name grammars whose
// homes live in `compiler/typecheck.vl`: it republishes them for `emit_classify`/
// `emit_collect`/`emit_mono`/`emit_rep`/`emit_rewrite`/`emit_sections` while calling
// them itself. Until `export … from` registered (#1196), each one had to be a one-line
// delegating function instead.
import { alsoPassed, midCalls, midCallsReexported, passed } from "./mid"

print(passed(21))
print(alsoPassed(21))
print(midCalls(2))
print(midCallsReexported(21))
